Based on the visual analysis of the chest X-ray, specifically focusing on the upper left lung field (which appears on the right side of the image due to standard radiological orientation), there is a distinct, thin white line visible parallel to the inner aspect of the ribs. This line represents the visceral pleura, separated from the parietal pleura by a small amount of air (pneumothorax). The absence of vascular markings peripheral to this line further confirms its identity as the edge of the collapsed lung.

Therefore, the outline of the visceral pleura is visible in the upper left lung field.

